Second Session

Discourse:
"The Sage's Position and Heavenly Virtue."

Edict, Mandate, and Memorial (tested on one):
Imitation of an Edict by Emperor Wen of Han Rejecting a Thousand-Li Horse;
Imitation of a Tang Mandate Appointing Lu Zhi 陸贄 as Hanlin Academician;
Imitation of a Memorial by Hu Anguo 胡安國 of the Song Presenting His Commentary on the Spring and Autumn Annals (Chunqiu zhuan).

Legal Judgments (five items):
Collecting Excess Grain Tax Above the Measure;
Clothing and Dwellings Violating Regulations;
Defrauding Officials or Private Persons to Obtain Property;
Administering Punishments Not in Accordance with the Law;
Failing to Drill and Train Soldiers.

Third Session

Five Policy Questions

Question: We reverently reflect that the Grand Ancestor, Emperor Wen (Taizong Wen Huangdi 太宗文皇帝, the Yongle Emperor), in his leisure from a myriad state affairs, selected one hundred and sixty-five people of antiquity who did good and received recompense, and recorded them in a book titled Secret Blessings for Doing Good (Weishan yinzhi 為善陰隲), graciously bestowing it upon his officials and people—truly a great treasure for ten thousand generations. Among them, …

Second Session.

Discourse:
The Sage's Position and Heavenly Virtue.

Edict, Mandate, and Memorial [examinees are to compose one]:
Imitation of an Edict by Emperor Wen of Han Declining the Thousand-Li Horse;
Imitation of a Tang Mandate Appointing Lu Zhi陆贄 as Hanlin Academician;
Imitation of a Memorial by Hu Anguo胡安國 of the Song Presenting the Commentary on the Spring and Autumn Annals (Chunqiu zhuan).

Legal Judgments [five items]:
Collecting excess grain tax above the rim of the measure;
Clothing and housing in violation of regulations;
Defrauding officials or private individuals to obtain property;
Administering punishments not in accordance with the law;
Failing to drill and train soldiers.

Third Session.

Five Policy Questions.

Question: We reverently reflect that the Grand Ancestor, Emperor Wen (Taizong Wen Huangdi), in his leisure from the myriad affairs of state, selected one hundred and sixty-five men of antiquity who performed good deeds and received recompense, recorded them in a book entitled Secret Merits of Doing Good (Weishan yinzhi), and graciously bestowed it upon his officials and people—truly a great treasure for ten thousand generations. Among them…
